WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.602
-Our first guests tonight
are Tony Award-winning actors

00:00:03.602 --> 00:00:05.672
who currently star in
"The Music Man,"

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:08.040
which is playing at the Winter
Garden Theatre on Broadway.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.009
Please welcome back to the show
Sutton Foster

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:11.878
and Hugh Jackman, everybody.

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.813
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.813 --> 00:00:22.755
♪♪

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:31.664
♪♪

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:34.701
Welcome! It's so lovely
to have both of you here!

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:37.303
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:38.638
I was -- I was lucky enough

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:41.674
to see this incredible musical
on opening night.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:43.743
There was such enthusiasm
because it's also --

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:45.644
It's a show, it's Broadway.

00:00:45.644 --> 00:00:48.748
It's people being
back out together again.

00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:50.416
Is that energy the same
every night?

00:00:50.416 --> 00:00:52.618
Because it was tremendous
the first night out.

00:00:52.618 --> 00:00:53.919
-It really has been.

00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:58.491
I mean, Broadway was nonexistent
for almost two years.

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:01.694
And I feel like there's this
shared sense of gratitude

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:03.996
between the people on stage
and the audience.

00:01:03.996 --> 00:01:06.398
And the show is so joyful,
and I'm having

00:01:06.398 --> 00:01:08.601
the time of my life
playing opposite this guy.

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.236
-You had to say that,
but thank you.

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:12.038
-I did!
But it's really like --

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:13.439
It's like --
It's a dream come true.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:15.241
-Can you guys clock
what kind of audience

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:16.576
you have on any given night?

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:17.810
I mean, you obviously have a lot

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.512
of two-person scenes
in the show.

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:22.014
Can you make eye contact and
know it's this kind of night?

00:01:22.014 --> 00:01:24.250
-Mm-hmm.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.186
-There's a couple of things.
So I'm on stage --

00:01:26.186 --> 00:01:27.854
Sutton's not on stage
at the beginning,

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:29.656
but I'm on stage
behind the thing.

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:32.725
And so sometimes you can hear
clapping along with the overture

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:35.561
and we have a little signal,
we can tell,

00:01:35.561 --> 00:01:39.598
but sometimes the audience
goes -- [Whistles] way off.

00:01:39.598 --> 00:01:41.333
Like, hecklers.

00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:42.902
-Oh, yeah, we did have --

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:45.405
-What kind of heckles do you
get during "The Music Man"?

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:47.474
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.474 --> 00:01:50.176
-Good point.
Mainly -- Mainly clean.

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:51.644
-Yeah, yeah.
-"Kiss her already!"

00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:53.479
-Oh, a lot "kiss her already"?
-Yeah.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:55.648
-You do make us wait for it
a bit in the show.

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:57.617
-Yeah, we do.
-We once got a, "Hey-oh!"

00:01:57.617 --> 00:02:00.253
Yeah, that was --
I'm not encouraging heckling.

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:01.554
-Yeah, yeah. Right. Exactly.

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:03.523
-I did -- I stand up
at the beginning of the show,

00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:07.292
I say one line, and I stand up
and I did hear a "Wolverine!"

00:02:07.292 --> 00:02:10.229
And I'm like...

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:13.632
"It's gonna be a long night,
pal, I'm just sayin'."

00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:15.034
-And they're like,
"He's not wolverine."

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:16.836
-There's not much
of that coming. -Yeah.

00:02:16.836 --> 00:02:18.905
He's, like -- The whole show
is about a con man.

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:21.207
That might be the twist at
the end, that it was Wolverine.

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:22.475
-Ooh.
-Yeah.

00:02:22.475 --> 00:02:24.477
-"Music Man Deux."

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:26.512
-But we did have
a hilarious sneezer.

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:28.114
-Oh, no.
-Yeah, it was like --

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:29.849
Someone with
a really bad allergy

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:31.784
who probably also
is a stand-up comedian

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:33.986
because the timing was perfect
on every sneeze.

00:02:33.986 --> 00:02:35.555
-Perfect timing.
And then this one --

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:38.224
We're in the second act.
We have a very long scene

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:40.459
together where we're like,
you know,

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:42.995
breaking down and starting to,
like, fall for each other,

00:02:42.995 --> 00:02:45.398
and this person
is sneezing repeatedly.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:48.668
And then at one point he went,
"Bless you."

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:50.837
And I was like, "Oh,"
and then it was over.

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:52.404
And then he exits
and just leaves me

00:02:52.404 --> 00:02:54.073
on stage to have to
endure the wrath.

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:56.543
-At which point, as I walked
out, Sutton goes, "Terrific."

00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:57.910
-"Great."

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.845
-"Never working with you again.
This is great."

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:02.415
-I mean, again, there are all
these tremendous moments,

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:04.517
but -- and beautiful songs.

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:07.553
But speaking of live theater,
there's a scene in a library

00:03:07.553 --> 00:03:09.221
where you have so many dancers

00:03:09.221 --> 00:03:12.358
and it's just the amount
of choreographed book throwing.

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:14.460
And, I mean,
I was there opening night.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:16.028
I did not see a book fall.

00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:17.296
Do books ever fall?

00:03:17.296 --> 00:03:18.664
-We were good on opening night.
-Yeah.

00:03:18.664 --> 00:03:19.999
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:22.701
-But I actually gave a hard hat
to the conductor

00:03:22.701 --> 00:03:25.204
because I nearly clocked him
about three nights ago.

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:28.041
-Really?
-I threw it up, and I lost it

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:31.043
in the light,
and I'm like, "Uh-oh."

00:03:31.043 --> 00:03:32.178
And I -- But I missed it.

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:33.579
And it was literally -- whoom!

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:35.214
But we have a musical --

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:36.816
Patrick Vaccariello
has got incredible timing

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:38.617
because it just went -- wheeoop!

00:03:38.617 --> 00:03:41.120
-Oh, he hit it back up!
-It was pretty great.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.355
-Have you ever, either of you,
personally been hit with a book?

00:03:43.355 --> 00:03:45.090
-Oh, I have, yeah.
I've got pelted in the head.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:46.159
Oh, yeah. Yeah, yeah.

00:03:46.159 --> 00:03:47.594
-Wow.
-They fly all over the place!

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:49.829
Then one night it kind of, like,
bounced and it went, ga-goink,

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:52.531
and I was like, "Okay."

00:03:52.531 --> 00:03:54.300
-So you -- I know you guys
had met over the years.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:57.203
Obviously, this is the first
time you're working together.

00:03:57.203 --> 00:03:59.605
I would imagine you each have
a different reason

00:03:59.605 --> 00:04:02.474
to be intimidated going into
rehearsals with one another.

00:04:02.474 --> 00:04:04.610
Was -- Did that exist
for each of you?

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:05.778
I mean --
-Yes.

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:08.114
-Yes, very much so.
I was -- Yes, yes.

00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:10.550
I mean, you're -- Yes.
You're Hugh Jackman.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:13.419
-So they told you right away
he was Hugh Jackman?

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.921
-Yeah, they said,
"It's Hugh Jackman."

00:04:15.921 --> 00:04:17.523
And then it's, like, our names

00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:20.192
are, like, really big
on, like, a giant billboard.

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:22.695
And I was like, "Oh, God, that's
gonna cost a lot of money

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:24.964
when they have to replace me."

00:04:24.964 --> 00:04:26.332
Because I'm like,
"That's real big.

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:28.668
It's a lot of paint."
But I was pretty convinced

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:31.003
I was going to be fired
for several weeks.

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:32.738
-Oh, stop it.
-It's true, though, you know?

00:04:32.738 --> 00:04:34.640
You just get -- But I guess,
like, I don't know,

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.143
that's part of the reason
why you do what you do.

00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:38.544
If you were like,
"Oh, yeah, I got this."

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:41.480
-But that's a big --
Has it always been that way?

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.584
I mean, you know, I know you did
"Boy from Oz" in 2004.

00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:46.185
I imagine
in everything you've done,

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:47.987
I imagine the early days
of the rehearsal,

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:49.422
you must always feel that, like,

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:51.724
"Oh, they're going to
pull the plug."

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:53.526
-And particularly
after the pandemic.

00:04:53.526 --> 00:04:55.961
It'd been a while for anybody
being back in that room.

00:04:55.961 --> 00:04:58.364
I was certainly intimidated.
This is a six-time Tony nominee.

00:04:58.364 --> 00:04:59.832
-Yeah.
-Two-time winner.

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:01.834
Like, I saw her do
"Thoroughly Modern Millie"

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:04.403
when you were like 4 years old.

00:05:04.403 --> 00:05:07.139
But I've watched everything
she's done. It's amazing.

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:08.841
And I'm the guy who,
in my first show,

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:10.275
"Beauty and the Beast,"
it was in my contract

00:05:10.275 --> 00:05:12.011
that I had to have
singing lessons once a week.

00:05:12.011 --> 00:05:13.379
So, you know, I --

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:14.647
[ Laughter ]

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:16.281
That's true, by the way.

00:05:16.281 --> 00:05:18.617
It may be a professional first.

00:05:18.617 --> 00:05:20.686
"Did you go this week?"
I'm like, "Yeah."

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:22.255
"Really?"
I was like, "yeah."

00:05:22.255 --> 00:05:24.823
-Did your teacher have to, like,
sign a note for you?

00:05:24.823 --> 00:05:26.258
-100%.
-That's really funny.

00:05:26.258 --> 00:05:28.794
Well, it's paid off, Hugh,
because you're absolutely --

00:05:28.794 --> 00:05:30.630
So you know what?
That was very unfair

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:33.432
that they made you do that,
but it was worth every penny.

00:05:33.432 --> 00:05:36.168
We'll be right back.
I have more to ask you guys.

00:05:36.168 --> 00:05:38.404
That's Sutton Foster and
Hugh Jackman. Stick around.

